    <main class="article">
      <a class="back" href="/">Back to homepage</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">Cloud Networking</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Hybrid cloud networking is not just about building a tunnel to AWS. A good design has to make routing, segmentation, security, observability, and operations work together.
      </p>

      <h2>Define the Traffic Model</h2>
      <p>
        Before choosing Direct Connect, VPN, Transit Gateway, or VPC Peering, I start with traffic patterns. Which applications need private access? Which systems still live in the data center? Is traffic mostly north-south, east-west, or branch-to-cloud? The design should follow the application model instead of forcing applications to follow the network diagram.
      </p>

      <h2>Plan Routing Boundaries</h2>
      <p>
        BGP is usually the cleanest way to exchange routes between on-prem and AWS, but route scope needs discipline. I avoid advertising everything everywhere. Production, staging, shared services, and security zones should have clear route boundaries, and summarization should be used where possible to keep routing tables understandable.
      </p>

      <h2>Use Segmentation Early</h2>
      <p>
        Security groups, NACLs, route tables, firewall policies, and identity controls should be part of the initial design. If segmentation is delayed until after migration, the cloud network often becomes a flat extension of the data center, which increases operational and security risk.
      </p>

      <h2>Design for Failure</h2>
      <p>
        A hybrid design must answer simple failure questions: What happens if a Direct Connect link goes down? What happens if a firewall pair fails? Does VPN backup take over automatically? Are route priorities deterministic? Can DNS still resolve critical services during partial failure?
      </p>

      <h2>Make It Observable</h2>
      <p>
        Flow logs, firewall logs, route table snapshots, CloudWatch metrics, provider circuit status, and on-prem monitoring should be correlated. When a service owner reports a timeout, the network team should be able to trace whether the problem is routing, security policy, DNS, load balancing, or application behavior.
      </p>

      <h2>Operational Discipline</h2>
      <p>
        The best hybrid cloud design is boring during daily operations. Naming conventions, route ownership, change review, rollback plans, and diagrams matter. They make the network easier to operate after the original design team moves on.
      </p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

